          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "MED",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation-usage.ts:1",
            "message": "recordInvestigationUsage (and its callee observationFor) have no recorded callers anywhere in the Story's changed symbol set — runInvestigationContinuation only calls progress.ts's BlockProgressCoordinator.account for usage accounting, never recordInvestigationUsage. This looks like a new usage-observation path that was added but never wired into the main continuation loop, so elapsed active-time usage may not actually be recorded via this mechanism. Note the companion continuation-usage.test.ts also shows no resolved call edges, which can mean the graph grounding is simply hollow for this just-created file rather than proof the code is truly dead — verify by reading continuation.ts's body and running the test file directly before concluding either way.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "LOW",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation.ts:1",
            "message": "The module-level check() function in continuation.ts (called from runInvestigationContinuation) and InvestigationNavigation.check() in navigation.ts (called from capability()) share the identical name 'check' but perform different checks (one appears to be an abort/turn guard, the other a ledger-drift guard against basis()). Reusing the same bare name for two semantically different guards in collaborating modules makes call sites harder to trace at a glance; a more specific name on one of them (e.g. assertNotAborted vs assertLedgerFresh) would remove the ambiguity. This is a naming/readability preference, not a functional defect.",
            "counterpartRef": "InvestigationNavigation.check",
            "confidence": "observation"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "LOW",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/continuation.ts:1",
            "message": "runInvestigationContinuation directly orchestrates roughly a dozen distinct collaborators (abort racing, capability/admission, seed/digest building, binding assertion, progress accounting/acceptance, pause/reply checks, context lookup). Even with the logic correctly delegated to each owning module, the sheer fan-out from one function makes its control flow harder to follow at a glance; consider whether the first-turn vs continuation-turn setup (admitInvestigationRequest/buildInvestigationSeed/investigationRequestDigest/assertInvestigationBindings) can be collapsed behind a single helper to shrink the orchestrator's visible surface. Flagged as a readability observation, not a confirmed defect, since the actual branching structure isn't visible from the symbol summary alone.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          }
        ]
      },
